# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Required
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Postgres connection string
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/state_of_health

# USDA FoodData Central. usda.service throws if this is unset — food search
# and AI grounding both fail without it. https://fdc.nal.usda.gov/api-key-signup
USDA_API_KEY=

# OpenRouter, used by both AI endpoints (/macros/estimate, /macros/label-scan)
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# Base64 of the Firebase service account JSON. Optional locally — dev falls
# back to ./serviceAccountKey.json. Required in deployed environments:
#   base64 -i serviceAccountKey.json
FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=

# USDA_API_KEY and OPENROUTER_API_KEY keep their existing runtime roles —
# /api/macros/estimate and /api/macros/label-scan call OpenRouter,
# /api/macros/search-branded-foods calls USDA — and are additionally required
# by the offline catalog scripts below. Once the catalog is seeded, plan
# generation, swaps, grocery aggregation, recipe viewing and internal catalog
# search make no live USDA or model calls.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development runtime and feature controls — active values, because a local
# checkout wants them set rather than discovered. Every value in this block
# is also what the code does when its line is absent, so deleting one
# degrades to that behaviour rather than failing — with ONE exception,
# restated on the key itself: CATALOG_MODEL_CALL_BUDGET has no default. It
# is a spending cap, and getCatalogModelCallBudget in scripts/lib/budget.ts
# has no safe number to invent, so it refuses a missing, blank, non-numeric
# or non-positive value and catalog:generate and catalog:validate — its only
# two readers — abort at startup rather than degrading.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Development values. Production sets NODE_ENV to production through the image
# (Dockerfile) and CI sets it to test; PORT is optional either way because
# src/server.ts already falls back to 3000.
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3000

# Size of this process's Prisma connection pool, appended to DATABASE_URL as
# connection_limit by src/prisma/client.ts. Must be a positive integer; the
# client refuses a value that is not one rather than guessing. A URL that
# already carries connection_limit wins, so an operator can size one
# deployment without changing code.
#
# Set explicitly because Prisma's own default is physical_cores * 2 + 1, which
# on a large host is 113 — more than a default PostgreSQL's max_connections of
# 100 all by itself — and the symptom is not a warning but HTTP 500s carrying
# Prisma P2037 "Too many database connections opened" the moment traffic
# bursts. Size it as max_connections divided by the number of processes
# sharing the server, leaving headroom for migrations, the catalog scripts and
# a psql session: 10 across two API replicas uses 20 of 100. Throughput per
# process is pool / p95-query-seconds, so 10 sustains roughly 65 searches a
# second on the measured p95 — the bound to raise only when that ceiling is
# the thing in the way.
DATABASE_CONNECTION_LIMIT=10

# Meal-planning server kill switch: off unless the value is exactly "true", so
# absent, blank or misspelled all mean off (the inverse of AI_FEATURES_ENABLED,
# which disables only on exactly "false"). Production turns it on only after
# the catalog, recipes and search benchmark have been loaded and verified.
# Gates /meal-planning/* and /recipes/* but never /meal-planning/targets* or
# /catalog/*, so Account, Diary and Progress keep reading and writing targets
# while planning is off.
MEAL_PLANNING_ENABLED=false

# Fault injection for reaching the failure states in development and test:
# off | generation | swap | log. Never set it in production — it is forced to
# "off" whenever NODE_ENV is production, so a stray value cannot take the
# service down, and an unrecognised value fails startup everywhere else.
MEAL_PLANNING_FAULT=off

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Request throttling (abuse control)
#
# Three in-process fixed-window buckets, mounted in src/app.ts after /health
# so the liveness probe is exempt by position: a process-wide ceiling ahead of
# every body parser, a bucket on the one unauthenticated write
# (POST /api/user), and a per-principal bucket behind the authentication
# boundary. src/services/rateLimit.logic.ts holds the arithmetic and the
# defaults; src/middleware/rateLimit.ts holds the store.
#
# Every key below is OPTIONAL and every value shown is what the code does when
# the line is absent — but a key that IS set and cannot be read as the integer
# it names fails startup rather than falling back, so a limit nobody chose can
# never be the one in force (the same rule as DATABASE_CONNECTION_LIMIT above).
#
# Throttling is DISABLED whenever NODE_ENV=test, so the test suite and its
# deliberate concurrency cases are never throttled. The limiter's own suite
# injects explicit policies to observe a refusal.
#
# A refusal answers 429 with {"error":"rate_limited", policy, limit,
# retryAfterSeconds, resetsAt}, carries Retry-After / RateLimit-Limit /
# RateLimit-Remaining / RateLimit-Reset / RateLimit-Policy, and logs one
# warn-level line: [meal-planning] rate_limit_refused {...}. The line carries
# no path, no token subject and no client address.
#
# The counters live in THIS process, so N replicas enforce N times each limit.
# That is deliberate — it adds no dependency and fails open — and it is why
# every number here is an abuse ceiling rather than a per-user quota. The AI
# daily quota, which is metered per user, stays in the database.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Set to exactly "false" to turn all three buckets off (the polarity of
# AI_FEATURES_ENABLED). Any other value, or unset, leaves them on outside the
# test environment.
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false

# The process-wide ceiling: requests per window across ALL callers, charged
# before any body is parsed. 1200/60s is 20 requests a second sustained, which
# leaves the recorded legitimate bursts (40 authenticated requests in 0.6 s, 8
# simultaneous grocery toggles) untouched by an order of magnitude.
# RATE_LIMIT_GLOBAL_MAX=1200
# RATE_LIMIT_GLOBAL_WINDOW_SECONDS=60

# Extra tokens a request answered 401 costs the global bucket, charged after
# the response. 29 makes a failed authentication cost 30, so ~40 of them in a
# minute exhaust the ceiling and an unlimited stream of 401s terminates in a
# 429 (CWE-307) — while a client whose token has merely expired pays only the
# one ordinary token for a normal answer. Set it to 0 to keep the ceiling and
# drop the penalty; raise the ceiling if legitimate 401 churn trips it, since
# this bucket is shared by every caller.
# RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_FAILURE_SURCHARGE=29

# Unauthenticated account creation on POST /api/user — the only unauthenticated
# write in the service. 10/60s is still 14,400 a day, far above any legitimate
# signup rate, and low enough that a scripted burst is refused partway through.
# RATE_LIMIT_SIGNUP_MAX=10
# RATE_LIMIT_SIGNUP_WINDOW_SECONDS=60

# Per verified token subject, behind the authentication boundary. 300/60s is 5
# requests a second for one principal.
# RATE_LIMIT_PRINCIPAL_MAX=300
# RATE_LIMIT_PRINCIPAL_WINDOW_SECONDS=60

# How many proxies THIS deployment owns, counted back from the end of
# X-Forwarded-For. 0 — the default — disables per-IP keying entirely, which is
# the only safe setting until the hop count is known: `trust proxy` is unset
# (src/app.ts), the proxy in front of this service APPENDS to the header, so
# its FIRST value is attacker-supplied and req.ip is the proxy's own address
# for every production request. With 0, unauthenticated traffic shares one
# global bucket instead of being keyed on an address that either belongs to the
# attacker or to the whole user base at once. Set it to the real number of
# proxies (1 behind a single reverse proxy) to key unauthenticated traffic per
# client address; the value read is then the one that many hops from the right,
# which is the last address this infrastructure wrote.
# RATE_LIMIT_TRUSTED_PROXY_DEPTH=0

# Upper bound on distinct keys one bucket's store may hold. Elapsed windows are
# swept before the bound is reached; only the per-principal bucket has real
# cardinality, and its keys are verified token subjects, which a client cannot
# mint. An eviction is reported once per window as rate_limit_store_pressure.
# RATE_LIMIT_MAX_TRACKED_KEYS=50000

# Catalog pipeline. Read only by the offline catalog and recipe seed scripts,
# never on a request path.

# Models for catalog generation and its advisory review pass. Leave blank to
# inherit OPENROUTER_MODEL and ESTIMATE_JUDGE_MODEL respectively — both are in
# the Optional section below, with the default each falls back to.
CATALOG_GENERATION_MODEL=
CATALOG_REVIEW_MODEL=

# USDA FoodData Central allows 1,000 requests per hour per key. 900 leaves 100
# per hour of headroom for the running API's estimate and branded-search
# traffic on the same key; the import pauses when the bucket empties rather
# than failing.
#
# 900 is an ENFORCED CEILING, not just this file's default: a value above it is
# REFUSED AT STARTUP, before any request is paced, and the importer reports the
# refusal as the gap code usda_rate_limit_misconfigured. Raising this to 1,000
# does not buy a faster import — it aborts the run, because those top 100 per
# hour are the live API's share of the same key. Only a lower value is
# accepted, and it simply paces the import more gently; unsetting the variable
# takes the ceiling as the default.
USDA_IMPORT_RATE_LIMIT_PER_HOUR=900

# Candidates per generation batch.
CATALOG_BATCH_SIZE=25

# Required positive integer, and ONE allowance per coverage-plan version —
# not per run. catalog:generate and catalog:validate --review reserve against
# the same number, and it is consumed cumulatively across every run, retry
# and resume of that coveragePlanVersion, so re-running a stage without
# raising this value does not hand it a fresh allowance. At the cap a stage
# stops with budget_exhausted; the ways forward are raising this number or
# publishing a new coveragePlanVersion, which is new work with an allowance
# of its own. That shared scope is what makes generation's startup estimate
# meaningful: it gates the coverage plan's modelCallsPerBatch × total batches
# (currently 2 per batch — one generation call, one advisory-review call)
# against this single number, which is the whole pipeline's cost rather than
# one stage's.
#
# Both stages resolve it AT STARTUP and fail closed when it is missing,
# blank, non-numeric, zero or negative — deliberately, and for both of them,
# because a spend cap is configuration rather than an argument: a checkout
# that has not set one is misconfigured whether or not the pass in front of
# it reaches a paid call (AAP §0.4.3, "the scripts fail closed at startup").
# Only the SPENDING is conditional: validation makes no model call without
# --review, and it is OPENROUTER_API_KEY, not this variable, that --review
# alone adds as a prerequisite. A call is reserved before it is made, so a
# failed call still consumes its reservation. scripts/lib/budget.ts owns the
# accounting and states the scope rule in full.
CATALOG_MODEL_CALL_BUDGET=1500

# Total timeout for one identity-evidence fetch.
EVIDENCE_FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS=10000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional — every value below has a working default; listed so the knobs are
# discoverable. Uncomment only to override.
#
# PORT appears twice on purpose: as the commented reference entry below, and as
# an active development value in the block above. The active assignment is the
# one a copied .env uses; src/server.ts still falls back to 3000 when neither
# is present.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Port the server listens on.
# PORT=3000

# USDA API root.
# USDA_BASE_URL=https://api.nal.usda.gov/fdc/v1

# Model used to estimate food items from text/photo input.
# OPENROUTER_MODEL=google/gemini-2.5-flash

# Model used by the USDA grounding judge (a classification task, so it gets its
# own model rather than reusing OPENROUTER_MODEL).
# ESTIMATE_JUDGE_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o-mini

# Set to exactly "off" to skip USDA grounding and return raw model estimates.
# Any other value (or unset) leaves grounding enabled.
# ESTIMATE_GROUNDING=off

# Kill switch. Set to exactly "false" to reject all AI calls, authoritative
# even against callers holding a valid token. Any other value leaves AI on.
# AI_FEATURES_ENABLED=false

# Free AI calls per user per UTC day, shared by estimate and label-scan.
# AI_DAILY_QUOTA=5

# Comma-separated emails exempt from the daily quota.
# AI_UNLIMITED_EMAILS=

# GIT_SHA is not set here — the Dockerfile injects it from the SOURCE_COMMIT
# build arg and /health reports it.

# Deliberately not a key here: SHADOW_DATABASE_URL, needed only by
# prisma migrate dev --create-only and prisma migrate diff. Both reset the
# database they point at, and a test run truncates its own, so give
# development, test and shadow three separate local databases and never point
# any of them at data you want to keep.

# The test suite's truncate authorisation, and the reason it is a variable
# rather than something the suite decides for itself: src/__tests__/setup
# empties the meal-planning tables plus meal_entries, meals and users
# (CASCADE) between suites, and nothing should be able to do that to a database
# by accident. The guard refuses to run at all unless all three of these hold:
# NODE_ENV is exactly "test", ALLOW_DB_TRUNCATE is exactly "true" ("TRUE", "1"
# and a blank value are refused), and DATABASE_URL names a database whose name
# ends in _test — optionally with a clone index, as in soh_test_46 — or is
# exactly "ci", on localhost, 127.0.0.1 or postgres. The name rule holds
# regardless of the other two: setting this variable can never authorise a
# truncate of a development, shadow or production database, and a _test name on
# a remote host is refused as well. Never set it in a shell that points at data
# anyone wants to keep.
#
# The key below is deliberately left commented, and not because an active value
# would be dangerous — the name rule above means this acknowledgement can never
# authorise a development, shadow or production database on its own. It is
# commented because putting it in .env would not work: the guard runs as Jest's
# first setup statement and reads the process environment directly, and nothing
# in the test harness loads this file, so a value written here is never seen by
# npm test. It belongs in the environment of the command itself:
#
#   NODE_ENV=test ALLOW_DB_TRUNCATE=true \
#     DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@localhost:5432/state_of_health_test \
#     npm test
#
# CI does exactly that, as job-level environment (see .github/workflows/ci.yml)
# against its own throwaway "ci" database.
# ALLOW_DB_TRUNCATE=true
